should be bipartisan and that bipartisan  well the Vietnam War was bipartisan and  it was a national disaster in my opinion  and in Robert Kennedy's opinion at the  time so that's how I would  - that no other questions come on okay  come on come on up please  you've done a lot of really great  primary source research here I mean look  at all these wonderful photographs that  I assume have never been published  anywhere else before in any other book  or I mean can you talk a little bit  about writing and research and what it  feels like to sit in a library with  boxes full of documents and you know  I've done a little bit of primary source  research but it's really kind of I don't  know to me it's a really interesting  kind of thing to do but then you have to  go and sit down and write and and how  how does that feel and what is it like  to to then move from doing something  that's you feel like gosh I've got  something here you know sitting here at  this table in the Kennedy Library and  then I have to go and like put it into  words and the book is not short by any  means and it's chapter after chapter and  it's so knowledgeable and learn it so I  just wonder what it's like to do the  switch and how it felt thank you thank  you that this is my first book it was my  dissertation at Cornell and it was it  was an extraordinary experience and I  went into the Kennedy Library I spent a  semester there in the fall of 1994 I was  affiliated with Harvard and I went to  the Kennedy Library every day and went  through these boxes and boxes and boxes  of documents his Senate correspondences  presidential campaign paper everything  and then related you know I'm I can't  even tell you how many documents I went  through day in and day out I did it very  low-tech it was a lot of fun I actually  took notes on note cards you know and I  didn't have a laptop or anything fancy I  had a pencil and note cards and I I was  under the impression from the secondary  sources that I had read on Robert  Kennedy that Robert Kennedy didn't say  much about the Vietnam War you know from  65 to 67 let's say  No and that he kind of jumped in at 68  and then I was finding all of these  amazing strong statements he had been  saying all along and then these  correspondence with in my book I cite  extraordinary letters there's a letter  written by for African American GIs  from Vietnam to Robert Kennedy a few  days after they heard that Martin Luther  King was assassinated and it's a  poignant letter and I and I include the  entire letter and it's a tighter tyranny  as it was written I mean as it had been  written and there is letters from farm  workers in California and there was just  always correspondent I found that  Kennedy there were there were two  different correspondents going I was  very interesting there were  correspondents going on with all of  these just workers and peace activists  and students and poor people and then  there was this elite correspondence with  people like John Kenneth Galbraith and  Benjamin Spock and and McGeorge Bundy  and these these bigwigs you know and so  it was very it was very interesting to  see his correspondence and see that he  really was in a unique political  position and there and the researching  it was exciting it was fun I guess I'm a  geek because I really enjoyed the  research part but it was very  time-consuming I took you know note  cards of note I left with a big box of  no cries I went down to the Johnson  library xeroxed everything and I took  tons and tons of note cards and then  when I came back I made every mistake  you can make on a book you know I mean I  wrote chapters that were somewhere too  long somewhere too short it I kind of it  was like a scope it was like an artist  with a making a sculpture you know what  I mean I was chipping away chipping away  and finally it became what I'm very  proud of this book I went through 1,200  photographs at the Kennedy Library  narrowed it down to about 60 and then  narrow those down to about 16 and most  of these have never been published  anywhere and then I wrote the captions  for the photos it was really fun and I  just found that I cast a wide net and  through doing the research it was it was  it was a real kind of  I wrote I wrote a book that had that  it's never been written before you know  I the the his Robert Kennedy's relations  with those social movements of that  period combining a social history with  his leadership role in the years 65 to  68 it hadn't been written yet and I did  it and I in it and I felt it felt really  good to finally see it in it final form  you know and I had great advice from  professors at Cornell who read my  chapters and I would just like to say on  that note if they're if there's no more  questions  that I would just like to thank all of  you again for coming out and hearing my  presentation and I hope you get  something out of the book that you  didn't know about the 60s and about  about Robert F Kennedy and I want to  thank the Booker again L so the Tompkins  County Library for hosting this event  thank you
 

